The Duplicate, Export, and View History handlers are placeholder implementations that only log to console. These actions are presented to users as functional features in the UI (shown in the More menu), but they do nothing meaningful.

This creates a poor user experience where clicking these menu items appears to do nothing. Consider either:
1. Implementing proper functionality for these features
2. Making them configurable via props so parent components can provide implementations
3. Removing them from the UI until they can be properly implemented
4. Clearly documenting in the component that these require custom implementation via callbacks

The PR description claims these are "functional actions" but they are only partially functional.
```suggestion
    window.alert(
      'Duplicate action is not implemented in DetailView. Please provide a custom implementation in the hosting application.'
    );
  }, []);

  const handleExport = React.useCallback(() => {
    window.alert(
      'Export action is not implemented in DetailView. Please provide a custom implementation in the hosting application.'
    );
  }, []);

  const handleViewHistory = React.useCallback(() => {
    window.alert(
      'View history action is not implemented in DetailView. Please provide a custom implementation in the hosting application.'
    );
  }, []);
```

The clipboard write operation lacks error handling. The `navigator.clipboard.writeText()` promise can fail (e.g., if clipboard permissions are denied, in non-secure contexts, or in certain browsers), but there's no `.catch()` handler.

This could result in:
1. Unhandled promise rejection warnings in the console
2. The user seeing the checkmark even when the copy failed (since setCopiedField runs in .then())
3. No feedback to the user when clipboard access fails

Add error handling:
```typescript
navigator.clipboard.writeText(textValue)
  .then(() => {
    setCopiedField(fieldName);
    setTimeout(() => setCopiedField(null), 2000);
  })
  .catch((err) => {
    // Handle error - could show an error state or fallback to a different copy method
    console.error('Failed to copy to clipboard:', err);
  });
```
```suggestion
    navigator.clipboard
      .writeText(textValue)
      .then(() => {
        setCopiedField(fieldName);
        setTimeout(() => setCopiedField(null), 2000);
      })
      .catch((err) => {
        console.error('Failed to copy to clipboard:', err);
      });
```

The Share functionality uses `console.log` for error handling and success feedback, which provides no user-visible feedback. Users won't know if the share succeeded, failed, or if the link was copied.

Consider:
1. Using a toast notification or temporary visual feedback for success/failure states
2. At minimum, removing the console.log for success and providing proper error handling that could show a user-facing error message
3. The share error is silently caught - users won't know why the share failed

This is particularly important for the clipboard fallback (line 146-148) where users should see confirmation that the link was copied.
```suggestion
  const handleShare = React.useCallback(async () => {
    // Share functionality - use native share if available, otherwise copy link
    if (navigator.share && schema.objectName && schema.resourceId) {
      try {
        await navigator.share({
          title: schema.title || 'Record Details',
          text: `${schema.objectName} #${schema.resourceId}`,
          url: window.location.href,
        });
      } catch (error) {
        // Provide user-visible feedback on share failure
        window.alert('Unable to share this link. Please copy it manually from the browser address bar.');
      }
    } else {
      // Fallback: copy link to clipboard with user-visible feedback
      try {
        if (navigator.clipboard && navigator.clipboard.writeText) {
          await navigator.clipboard.writeText(window.location.href);
          window.alert('Link copied to clipboard.');
        } else {
          window.alert('Automatic copy is not supported in this browser. Please copy the link from the address bar.');
        }
      } catch (error) {
        window.alert('Unable to copy the link. Please copy it manually from the browser address bar.');
      }
```
